# secrets
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# The captured corpus: ~370 MB of GitHub Actions logs across 43 runs.
# Deliberately not in git. It is reproducible — `ci-triage hunt` finds failed
# runs and `ci-triage fetch` re-captures any of them — so committing it would
# trade a 44 MB clone for bytes anyone can regenerate from the run URL.
#
# The labels are the exception, and they are tracked. A run URL regenerates its
# logs; nothing regenerates a human reading them and deciding what broke. They
# are the eval set's ground truth and they exist on exactly one laptop, so the
# four rules below keep every meta.json and not one log line.
#
# It takes four rather than one because git will not descend into an excluded
# directory: `fixtures/` followed by `!fixtures/*/meta.json` un-ignores nothing,
# since the parent is already gone. So the directories stay visible and their
# contents are excluded a level down instead.
fixtures/*
!fixtures/*/
fixtures/*/*
!fixtures/*/meta.json

# One small run (~430 KB) is tracked whole, because the test suite reads it and
# CI has no other way to get it. The zip duplicates the logs beside it.
!fixtures/sweep__30005725094/*
fixtures/sweep__30005725094/logs.zip

# Answered verdicts, keyed by prompt + model + fixture. A cache, not a result:
# re-running rebuilds it, and editing the prompt invalidates it automatically.
.triage-cache/

# Eval reports are the opposite: `reports/*.json` is a result, tied to one model
# and one prompt revision, and is what the numbers in the README are read from.
# It is tracked. The raw sweep logs beside it are not — they are a terminal
# transcript, regenerable by re-running the sweep against the cache for free.
reports/*.log

# Project planning notes, not documentation.
readme

# python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# macos
.DS_Store
